  "footer": "Agreeing on a Secret in Plain Sight · <a href=\"../../../\">visual.integ.life</a><br>Models on this page: multiplication mod n, inverses and orders (Fermat's little theorem, Euler's theorem); square-and-multiply (Knuth, <cite>The Art of Computer Programming</cite>, vol. 2, §4.6.3); baby-step giant-step (Shanks 1971; <cite>Handbook of Applied Cryptography</cite>, Algorithm 3.56); Diffie–Hellman (1976); RSA (1978) and the cube-root attack when e = 3; the elliptic-curve group law and the Hasse bound (Silverman, <cite>The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves</cite>, chapters III and V); SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) and hash-then-sign; the heuristic cost L<sub>n</sub>[1/3, (64/9)<sup>1/3</sup>] of the general number field sieve (with o(1) dropped) and Pollard ρ; the classical reduction of Shor's algorithm (Nielsen and Chuang, chapter 5); Mosca's inequality; Babai rounding (1986); Regev's LWE encryption (2005). All are small-parameter teaching versions.<br>Sources for the facts: NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Rev. 5; FIPS 203/204/205; NIST IR 8547 (2026); NIST IR 8610; the NIST press release on HQC (2025); IETF RFC 10024, RFC 8446, RFC 8017, RFC 7919, draft-cfrg-schwabe-kyber-04; Cloudflare blog (March 2024, September 2026) and developer docs; Google Security Blog; Signal and Apple security blogs; OpenSSH 10.0 release notes; Microsoft security blog; UK NCSC; the EU NIS Cooperation Group post-quantum workstream's coordinated roadmap (2025); the joint statement of 18 EU member-state agencies including BSI and ANSSI (2024); the NSA CNSA 2.0 FAQ v2.2 (Oct 2026); IACR ePrint 2020/697, 2022/214, 2022/975, 2024/555, 2025/1237; arXiv 2505.15917 and 2603.28846; the Cognition blog and saweis.net (Sep 2026); US Executive Order 14412 (<cite>Federal Register</cite>, Jun 2026); the Wikipedia “RSA numbers” entry (Nov 2026, a secondary source); Global Risk Institute (2026); liboqs parameter tables. Full links and access dates are in this page's BRIEF.md."
